g\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> A set with the same subset of poset.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/0bef98b3ecf9657beab37074dea83d81493f1817.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/ccb3deaa374a4ca3cac68854dcea6a9157fd9efd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":7,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider (<em>S</em>, ∗) is a semigroup where <em>S</em> = {<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>c</em>, <em>d</em>}. Assume <em>a</em> ∗ <em>b</em> = <em>c</em>, <em>b</em> ∗ <em>b</em> = <em>a</em> and <em>d</em> ∗ <em>a</em> = <em>b</em>. Then <em>d</em> ∗ <em>c</em> =</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>a</em></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>b</em></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>c</em></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of these</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/22f73eea4dc9ce672be5e6f12b16d89a30bda763.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":8,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>What is the converse of the following assertion?<br />\n“I can top the exam only if I study”</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> I can top the exam if I study</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> If I can top the exam then I study</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> If I do not study then I cannot top the exam</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> If I cannot top the exam then I study</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/3a59aee6fba3490108104fd75f802a2523cfc1ed.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":9,"type":"MSQ","correct":"B,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>A binary relation R defined on a set S is said to be antisymmetric if for x, y ∈ S,</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/d8032d2efe3f0fd4cecfbbcfc428cf83041b2456.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>Which of the following statements is/are true?</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> A binary relation cannot be both symmetric and antisymmetric.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> A binary relation can be both transitive and antisymmetric.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> The union of two equivalence relations is always an equivalence relation.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> The intersection of two equivalence relations is always an equivalence relation.</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b, d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/b18cb89b59c3ccc327695e9c277f3d852228ef70.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":10,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Let f be a function from set A to B. Consider the following statement:<br />\n<strong><em>P</em></strong> : For each <em>a</em> ∈ <em>A</em>, there exists unique <em>b</em> ∈ <em>B</em> such that <em>f</em>(<em>a</em>) = <em>b</em>.<br />\n<strong><em>Q</em></strong> : For each <em>b</em> ∈ <em>B</em>, there exists a ∈ A such that <em>f</em>(<em>a</em>) = <em>b</em>.<br />\n<strong><em>R</em></strong> : There exists <em>a</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>a</em><sub>2</sub> ∈ <em>A</em> such that <em>a</em><sub>1</sub> ≠ <em>a</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>f</em>(<em>a</em><sub>1</sub>) = <em>f</em>(<em>a</em><sub>2</sub>).<br />\nThe negation of the statement “<em>f</em> is one-one and onto” is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>P</em> or not <em>R</em></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>R</em> or not <em>P</em></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>R</em> or not <em>Q</em></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <em>P</em> and not <em>R</em></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8ed8391a74b0c3adfd5ccb7649478a5e28e0d713.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":11,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider a simple undirected graph <em>G</em>(<em>V</em>, <em>E</em>) with 102 nodes labelled V<sub>0</sub>, V<sub>1</sub>, V<sub>2</sub>, ..... V<sub>101</sub>. For node<br />\n<img src=\"/media/24434697c00b18fbf1f5ae2e7b6b587f25fd118b.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> Other than this,<br />\n(<em>V</em><sub>0</sub>, <em>V</em><sub>1</sub>) and (<em>V</em><sub>101</sub>, <em>V</em><sub>100</sub>) are in <em>E</em>. What is the chromatic number of graph <em>G</em>?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 1</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 2</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 3</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 4</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/2cecce44d94f6ad63556985ab6d29dd50a036086.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":12,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following predicates with the domain as set of integers for all variables.<br />\n<em>P</em>(<em>m</em>) : <em>m</em><sup>2</sup> > –1<br />\n<em>Q</em>(<em>m</em>) : <em>m</em><sup>2</sup> –6<em>m</em> + 8 = 0<br />\n<em>R</em>(<em>m</em>, <em>n</em>) : <em>m</em><sup>2</sup> = <em>n</em><br />\nWhich of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/336f044652c4a523955c4d63b90763a3f3634a2b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/d30c12bebdde5f953da0ab7a395ead8b911aba1b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/cfb7559fbb32c9bc7f5478624f92543bc0d49ace.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Both (a) and (c)</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/7dd440977c7e64ad1ddc44e5093a6f8ef7c3adc8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":13,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p><em>p</em>, <em>q</em> and <em>r</em> be propositions as given below:<br />\n<em>p</em> : Program requested a page<br />\n<em>q</em> : Page fault occurred<br />\n<em>r</em> : Error occurred<br />\nConsider a system with following specifications:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : “If program requested a page, then page fault occurred or error occurred”.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : “Error occurred if and only if program requested a page and page fault occurred”.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : “Error occurred”.<br />\nFor how many possible truth assignments to <em>p</em>, <em>q</em> and <em>r</em> such that the above system specifications are consistent?</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"1","high":"1"},"solution":"<p>1</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/94abeeff67134e8bcc03680def82f5c596082425.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":14,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>f</em> is a function from set of integers to the set of integers. Find which of the following function is neither one to one nor onto function.</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/d9f006d270f72bc2808e99287bfa5a3d8aff2cea.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/0f4688422ac0a57d473a7b9384c9c0fe511e9035.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/cd4474ee4cf1b360868e0d4d7e20fa6120d81847.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/56c7dcbdbbc1e90f4d19d3886b38f9fe2217f19c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4edd0d7a536111665899d6fee2946414368cddfd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":15,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Find the middle term in the expansion of <img src=\"/media/62ea2dceac293fa029418a3803bf105930cd5c92.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/14ec1fcab20b373d56de7eecf89c75e5ce5745d0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/a4057e413cf03a07592b6c323b582b2ddf60bea0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/cc4ab18cd0a3843cd7e4892285e3383b85be267e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/affd29ccb4f3dc8dc0aae3d34fafe850de3d1f37.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4121e4d07b4f3efaa94ac6dff8d5802af99116eb.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":16,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Let <img src=\"/media/884c08cfbaea254ce53dfa264e141749cb9281bd.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> be a group where G is a set and <img src=\"/media/2910116d7f6c82564ff6044996bad33e44fe09f0.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" />\u0001 is a binary operation on G. Consider G′ is a nonempty<br />\nfinite subset of G. And G′ is closed under \u0001<img src=\"/media/2910116d7f6c82564ff6044996bad33e44fe09f0.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> . Then which of the following is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/0b1740083bc7fd67a64145583da33a6aee15eec0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/bf0194886516d80b5273780ca536bfc710b5849c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> There may be an element in <em>G</em>′ whose inverse is not in <em>G</em>′.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/fd2681c3fa2a28578984eb41255be88fd7b193ef.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/708f08f69ddd7a73716e0dff4d52041de5472c71.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":17,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the directed graph given below:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/e10151f043f3f3e7a0c0843a29a5e4680f378785.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>Which of the following is TRUE?</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> The graph does not have topological ordering.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> DMACBEYS is one of the topological ordering.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> DMCABEYS is one of the topological ordering.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Both (a) and (c)</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nIn finding topological ordering of any graph<br />\n1. Choose vertex which has &lsquo;0&rsquo; indegree. If not present &lsquo;0&rsquo; indegree vertex then topological ordering not possible.<br />\n2. If &lsquo;0&rsquo; degree node present then, remove it and find next &ldquo;zero&rdquo; indegree vertex.<br />\n3. If &lsquo;0&rsquo; degree node present then, remove it and find next &ldquo;zero&rdquo; indegree vertex.<br />\n4. Topological ordering is : &ldquo;DMACBEYS&rdquo;.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":18,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider a complete undirected simple graph G with 4 nodes. Each vertex and each edge in <em>G</em> are labelled (different). How many different maximal matchings possible for above graph <em>G</em>?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 4</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 6</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 2</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 3</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/f5b658ff97ddcf4868176e65e01cda38fc2a640b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":19,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p><em>G</em> be a connected graph in which only one node have degree > ‘1’ and rest of nodes are of degree ‘1’. Add an edge between every two node of degree ‘1’ in such a way that if <em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>c</em>, <em>d</em> are node then <em>a</em> to <em>b</em> one edge, <em>b</em> to <em>c</em> one edge, <em>c</em> to <em>d</em> one edge and <em>d</em> to a one edge. The resultant graph is sure to be</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Regular</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Complete</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Hamiltonian</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Euler</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/9e6afba3ccd47e3ad77b8811ee89a4dd25a1c05d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":20,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,C,D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Let (G, *) be a group such that O(G) = 8, where O(G) denotes the order of the group G. Which of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> There exists no element a in G whose order is 6.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> There must exist an element a in G whose order is 4.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> There exists more than one element a in G such that O(a) = 1.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of these</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nTheorem: If a is an element of G, then O(a) must divide O(G).<br />\nTherefore (a) is true, as O(a) = 6 and 6 doesn&rsquo;t divide 8.<br />\nHowever the converse of the above theorem need not be true, therefore (b) is false.<br />\n(c) says there are at least two elements in G with order 1, however this violates the fact that every<br />\ngroup can have only one identity element. So (c) is also wrong. So the appropriate choice is (d).</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":21,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following predicates with the domain as set of integers for all variables.</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/1ddace1f7f2f6c8525a9aa0dee9a737edef59b1e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>Which of the following is true?</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/7ddfbc9db2005389596fe8d7d5985b33df5646be.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/23ae5b0270e24de54098bc0187fbe20dd2b3b2f4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/acb7cf42149e4cd3313b23070dd718ba32d73d37.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of these</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/a00fe74a70a3583b7c1637980ec2217be972e3d6.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":22,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Which of the following is/are false?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Set of integers on addition operation is Monoid but not group.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Set of integers on subtraction operation is Monoid but not group.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Set of integers on multiplication operation is group but not Abelian group.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of these</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)<br />\n(Z, +) is both group and Abelian group, as it satisfies commutative property and inverse element<br />\nis &ndash;a &forall;a &isin; Z.<br />\n(Z, &ndash;) is never semigroup, because subtraction operation is not associative and hence cannot be<br />\nmonoid too.<br />\n(Z, &times;) is monoid but not group, because inverse does not exist i.e. for any integer &lsquo;a&rsquo; it&rsquo;s inverse is<br />\n1/a which is a rational number.<br />\nHence it is monoid only.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":23,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : In a distributive lattice, if <img src=\"/media/3c8ce968c4df6b272a5f2c087494a748b1617a83.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> are elements of set of this distributive lattice.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : In a distributive lattice L, a given element a can have at most one complement.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/558a8639fea524affba4a821bab8a9aacf0b13fd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":24,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Which of the following is correct statement ?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Every distributive lattice is complemented lattice.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Every complemented lattice is distributive lattice.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Every distributive lattice is bounded.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of the above</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/6762e9ee265e4b1f2ce5aaba2bfe3777c9436a3f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":25,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>s</em> = {<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>c</em>} and <em>P</em>(<em>s</em>) is power set of s. <em>X</em> = (<em>P</em>(<em>s</em>), ⊆) is partially ordered set (poset) with partial order as ⊆. Which of the following is/are correct for poset <em>X</em>.</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Poset <em>X</em> is a lattice.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> For every element <em>z</em> of <em>P</em>(<em>s</em>), <em>s</em> – z is complement of z in poset <em>X</em>.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Poset <em>X</em> is a distributive lattice.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Hasse diagram of <em>X</em> has 9 edges.</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8a31bad4e0ffffefdac5ad66089d3e4f54cac6a2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/c15a6cc2a029ba2d232c49d4930d5af095fb0f37.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":26,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following graphs:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/318be44b1d21e592442aa300855776fe117eb674.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>The number of graphs which are Euler ________.</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>3</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>G</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>3</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of these</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/9d0fa69d6cab386375998ec3df5553253e3a7fda.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":27,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>In a college’s mathematics department, there are 7 women and 9 men. How many distinct ways can a committee of 5 members be formed such that the committee includes atleast one women and atleast one men?</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"4221","high":"4221"},"solution":"<p>4221</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/5c62129a0d58e34d3a1979228ef6b78fc150fe43.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":28,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Which of the following graphs have Hamiltonian cycle?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/9de2f26f566cc88ab2c7fa85f170745dc27d8954.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/515db21941116c34fd526831d95313154144e0b2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/70dc961ae65823157f3b954db8c9c8a6f086de66.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/8cb2cdb3c375bdfa61b206cd0839821aab6fafa3.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/7b792b9572d3cb278d26bd132c8b2a298e9cbc8a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":29,"type":"NAT","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The number of solutions are there to the equation <em>x</em><sub>1</sub> + <em>x</em><sub>2</sub> + <em>x</em><sub>3</sub> + <em>x</em><sub>4</sub> + <em>x</em><sub>5</sub> + <em>x</em><sub>6</sub> = 31 are ________.<br />\nWhere <em>x</em><sub>1</sub> ≥ 1, <em>x</em><sub>2</sub> ≥ 2, <em>x</em><sub>3</sub> ≥ 2, <em>x</em><sub>4</sub> ≥ 4, <em>x</em><sub>5</sub> ≥ 6, <em>x</em><sub>6</sub> ≥ 5?</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"4368","high":"4368"},"solution":"<p>4368</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/7c77872379a60bc1ec0d899fbf5628356ee2d07b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":30,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Let a set <em>S</em> defined as</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/61787916f65ab2e41f1b9f2384af24057349ed5b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>Find the cardinality of the set.</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 25</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 60</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 63</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 43</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/90ce78601584f911f3d681abcfd38bf1780507b3.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":31,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>D<sub>x</sub></em> = N (the set of natural numbers excluding zero) and <em>D<sub>y</sub></em> = <em>N</em><sup>0</sup> (the set of natural numbers<br />\ninclude zero). Define the predicate <em>P</em>(<em>x</em>, <em>y</em>) as ‘<em>x</em> divides <em>y</em>’.<br />\nWhich of these following quantified statement is/are true?</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4a4747226493aa60e8a37007f3ba553b4390c18b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Only 1 and 4 are true</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Only 1 and 3 are true</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Only 2 is true</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> All of them above</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/d5364ad59a00faa0b265b3a615ef9f792eee5d25.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":32,"type":"MSQ","correct":"C,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>For the composition table of a cyclic group shown below:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/e8a43eb0e720a683122e0776efbccd1678ac6260.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>Which one of the following is/are generators?</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>a</em></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>b</em></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>c</em></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <em>d</em></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c, d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/d65f15d1e61074edcd1bcbd8f8739e11a2c6eaf1.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":33,"type":"MSQ","correct":"B,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>M</em> denote the set of all lines in a plane. Let <em>R</em> and <em>S</em> be two relations on <em>M</em> defined such that,</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/e6653a603dfae3a712839b03eaeaede692056093.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>Then which of the following is correct about <em>R</em>, <em>S</em>, <em>T</em> and <em>U</em>?</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Both <em>R</em> and <em>S</em> are equivalence relations.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>R</em> is an equivalence relation, but <em>S</em> is not an equivalence relation.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>T</em> is antisymmetric relation .</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <em>U</em> is both asymmetric and antisymmetric relation.</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b, d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/fa18b74bbbe80d414b8ccf8e3fa458084761bed8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"}],"questionCount":33,"parsedAt":"2026-11-10T20:11:32.367Z"}